[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-40516"] :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-177950/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "5b3dc04c-bd8f-5561-be3b-0ed284e34c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177950/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "554920c6-385e-532f-9b99-a1167d813b0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177950/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "895a6e32-f6c1-5f0a-8c51-c9720068b325" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177950/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "8080f578-ff72-5244-94f8-3e740a493c65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177950/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "80954421-08eb-5a6e-8863-e0803e76872a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177950/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "89e69491-e242-5d53-a5d6-e44b49980bdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-177950/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-177950/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-177950/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-177950/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-177950/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-177950/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-177950" :question/id #uuid "96d393f1-651b-502e-b281-70311ebb53c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-177950" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-177950/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-177950"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-177769/selection/210215610152bc22dbe0958b830998dd4e50f2b6c40baf314cb7ba76c4d375cb" :answer/id #uuid "1d4373db-d69f-509f-b61a-9df6545acd6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177769/selection/faad0727b2c62b6cced88abf9b210ea8763c2fd1c03c01c27346d66484992037" :answer/id #uuid "391f14fe-8d35-5780-bf0f-1e3527dfcb0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177769/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "431792d9-57f8-526a-a852-d3884064b516" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177769/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "fc58a63c-67b1-53a6-bdee-629b4ae3bc44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177769/selection/4f68045571b86fba82e2a0c2725b3ed6a2ffd6ada3a3ea64f83eacafaa3139df" :answer/id #uuid "02f82e33-327d-5475-b8a5-804644130bb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177769/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e871d6-82d8-5e94-b3cc-3c21ef69a415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-177769/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-177769/selection/210215610152bc22dbe0958b830998dd4e50f2b6c40baf314cb7ba76c4d375cb" "q-177769/selection/faad0727b2c62b6cced88abf9b210ea8763c2fd1c03c01c27346d66484992037" "q-177769/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-177769/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-177769/selection/4f68045571b86fba82e2a0c2725b3ed6a2ffd6ada3a3ea64f83eacafaa3139df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-177769" :question/id #uuid "fb970341-2e72-50d6-9722-db7e72c25442" :question/math-academy-id "q-177769" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-177769/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-177769"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40513/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "bd70a2cc-cb0b-5865-ada5-de7c9c4d6498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40513/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "778720aa-11b6-5fbe-9b0e-f69c